[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Over the last 12 weeks, I\'ve taught 3,387 people to use AI, to become an AI first operator. We\'ve literally had that many people in our Slack channel, showing up twice a week to live sessions to go from being your everyday AI user, the 99% of'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'people who are just using it to work a little bit faster, maybe rewrite some emails, to become one of the top percent, 1% of AI users, an AI first operator. And we\'ve seen results like'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'people being able to take 30 hours worth of work and build systems using AI and cut their workload down to 2 hours a week. That\'s a literal transformation we\'ve had from one of our students. And'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for people who don\'t know, I am the AI curriculum and IP lead at a company called the Uncommon Business where we transform people from, you know, your average user into AI power users. And I\'ve been journaling on what I learned'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'over the last 12 weeks. The biggest mistakes that people make and the one big thing, the one power move they can actually take to go from that 99% to that 1% and uh and change their lives.'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Been sat here journaling on it while I actually waiting for the tide to go out before I go for a surf here at Par. I\'ve actually written down uh 13 different reasons here in my journal. And uh I\'m not going to go through all 13. I\'m'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to go through five. There\'s a little bit of a change of programming from my usual uh structured clean walkthrough demos on this channel. If you\'re new here, this is going to be a little bit more unstructured and we\'re going to go through this and I\'m going to tell you the mistakes people make and'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how you can take that mistake and transform it and become one of these top 1% AI users. And the first one I want to talk about, the biggest mistake, and I literally had this question on my my LinkedIn today, and it\'s to do with what'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I call tool paralysis. The biggest question that I get repeatedly is what tool should I should I be using? Is claude better than chat GPT? What about Gemini? Should I be using gro or perplexity? And this is a massive'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stumbling block that I see everyone make. And it\'s hard because you get overwhelmed and every week one is apparently better than the other. One has killed another one. And what the top people using AI understand is it\'s not really about the tool. Sure, one might'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'be a bit better than the other, but for the average user, they\'re missing the really critical part. Most people make the mistake of using chat GPT for a long while. They build up their memory in chat GPT and then they feel like they\'re'

[02:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stuck with that tool and should they switch to the next one. And that\'s not really how you should be building with AI. What I want you to understand is it\'s not about which tool is better. It\'s about the system you\'re building'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'underneath. And that means building really deep context. You\'ll hear context talked about a lot in AI and people will be like just create one file or just build up your memory over time. But actually to use AI effectively in your work, in your business, in your personal'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'life, you need to get everything out your head and you need to document it in depth. Most people only go surface level and they just write an about me file and it has a little bit of information. And sure, you need to create a file that is'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything about your work and about your business, but that is pretty uh surface level. You need to go way deeper. Once you\'ve documented all of that key stuff about your business, you then need to be going deeper. You need'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to be talking about your ICP, really dialing in your customer avatar so you know who you\'re talking to. You need to be building context about your pricing, your services, your financial goals, your team if you have one, all the'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different roles in your company, who reports to who, along with getting out of your brain. This is something that most businesses miss anyway, is all the step-by-step operating procedures, the standard operating procedures of how'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your business runs. This should all be documented anyway. But even more so in a world with AI, if you want AI to actually start doing work for you and save 10, 20, 30 hours a month and increase your profit margins, earn more'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'money, if you document step by step every process in your business, every system in your business into an operating procedure, that\'s a massive unlock for AI because then you can start feeding that information to AI and show it how you complete a task. It can learn'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that and then go and execute step by step for you. This is also how you escape that tool paralysis and should I be using this one versus that one? I actually build this all into like a second brain operating system in a'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'complete file structure and a system so that if I decide to go and use chat GPT work instead of claude coowork I can easily just go and switch platforms hook my second brain up to that and start working or I can go to perplexity or'

[04:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'grock or whatever new platform comes out. I\'m not locked to one platform because I\'ve built up memory in there. I\'ve actually built a system underneath that always knows what I do, where I am in particular projects, a complete'

[04:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memory that is agnostic to the actual tool we\'re using. And once you start to understand how to build that up, that\'s going to be a massive unlock so that every time you log into any tool, you can pick up a project where you left'

[04:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'off. for me and for our students, they can go from working in claude codework or claude code and switch over to chat GPT or codeex in a matter of seconds without losing any context or what'

[05:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they\'re doing. It also helps you with the the second point that I want to come on to which is I\'ve actually been reading a book Simon Synynic\'s book which is it\'s called start with why. If Simon has start with why, I have'

[05:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something called the the start with pain framework. And once you start documenting your business, you start to uncover all the pains in your business, the things that are causing you to suffer that you could be getting AI to do. Once you identify the the pain that'

[05:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re dealing with, you can then understand what causes that pain and then you can take that and go to AI. The one big thing that people don\'t understand about AI is let\'s take a detour into let\'s say you have a pile of'

[05:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dirty clothes. Well, you find a tool to actually clean your clothes. You go and get a washing machine and you take your pile of dirty clothes and you put them in the washing machine and hopefully they come out the other end and they\'re clean. You know what a washing machine'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'does? It cleans clothes. If the clothes come out dirty, you say, "Ah, that\'s a that\'s a rubbish tool. That\'s a crappy tool. Uh, it doesn\'t work or it\'s broken. I should get a new one." It\'s the same with how software, the whole software industry has been developed for'

[06:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the last 30 years. You find a pain that a consumer is struggling with and you build a software or a tool to solve that specific pain. Take I don\'t know Netflix that was designed so that you didn\'t'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have to go out to Blockbuster and rent a DVD. You didn\'t have to go down there and rent a DVD on a Friday night when it\'s raining. It was a pain for people. Netflix came along and said, "Hey, we\'re going to we\'re just going to bring that'

[06:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to your home." actually makes me think of um in the UK we had something called Love Film where they used to post DVDs to you and stuff which was a sort of a competitor to Netflix at the time but anyway obviously all that\'s now streamed'

[06:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'straight into your home but it solves that pain of going out into the rain on a Friday night it just comes to you ready to go that\'s the same for software as it\'s ever been find a pain and solve it the thing with AI is AI can solve any pain in your life it\'s designed to solve'

[07:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any problem which means Most people and myself included when I go to sit down at an AI tool, I need to come up with the pain. I need to come up with the thing, the cause of this pain in my life or my business and then work with AI to solve'

[07:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it. And that\'s really hard to do. That\'s a different way of thinking of using a tool for some people. So, most people sit down and think, well, it can write, so I\'ll get it to do writing jobs. But once you\'ve actually documented your'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'business and even written down on paper all the things you hate doing, we could go to emails again. You could say like, "Oh, I hate Sundays because I know Monday morning my my email inbox is going to be absolutely full and that\'s a'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'real pain for me. What is the cause of that?" Well, I don\'t have anyone managing my emails for me. So, we\'ve got a pain of I hate doing emails cuz it\'s busy work that is just not adding value to my life. Two, I\'ve got no one or'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nothing that actually does it automatically for me. Okay, great. Now, we understand that we\'ve got a pain there. we can take that, we can go to AI and start working out how to get it to automate clearing out some of your inbox for me. And that\'s I think a large part'

[08:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of the work that I do is helping people understand this and identify the pain starting with the pain in their life and their business and then figuring out how they can go and solve that problem or the cause of that pain using an AI tool.'

[08:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So once you have that massive context about everything you do and you start uncovering pains, things you don\'t like doing in your work, then you can take both of that and you can just literally'

[08:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go to Claude or chat GPT or whatever it is with all of this stuff and start figuring out even ask it like how can we solve this problem? How can you take this off my plate? Especially when you\'ve got an SOP step by step about how you complete that task. pretty much'

[08:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Claude chat GPT whatever can go in and start working out which tasks it can execute for you along that way that\'s how you start getting these huge time gains and sure the first time you do it in an AI tool it might take the same'

[08:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'amount of time or it might take longer than doing it manually which is where a lot of people give up they\'re like ah I couldn\'t really do it the first time so bad tool it doesn\'t work it\'s a bad washing machine it didn\'t clean my clothes and they give up but once you'

[09:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually get that dialed once you actually spend the time to teach AI like a a fresh in turn into your business. You\'ve given it the context and taught it how to do a job. Might take a while, but it takes it off your plate in the long run. That\'s how you get those'

[09:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'massive, massive upgrades in efficiency and taking that sort of time of hours to do a task down into automation or just a few minutes to get things done. Number three on my'

[09:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'list, transcripts. Voice is everything. This is the easiest unlock you can have. Easiest unlock you can have. Number one is actually start talking to your computer. Forget your fingers and typing'

[09:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things out. Using dictation to prompt and work back and forwards and have conversation with an AI tool. One, it allows you to voice type faster. I think like if you use a tool like Whisper Flow, it averages four or five times'

[09:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'faster in typing than using your fingers. The other thing you\'ll find is you get a massive amount more of detail into what you\'re doing because it\'s not a slog to write things out. You can just do what I am now. You can sit here and sort of brain dump into your tool and'

[10:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get a massive amount of context. Get all of that subconscious stuff off your off your chest and just stream of consciousness into AI to deliver that context or tell it how you usually execute a task for them to start working'

[10:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'back and figure out how AI can do that for you. On the flip side to that as well, transcripts. You should be recording every conversation, every call you have in your work and your business. all your sales calls, team meetings, one- on-ones, marketing events, because'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s where all the good stuff lives. That\'s where you can actually start helping develop tone of voice. You can really run your expertise through AI. Think about how much, think about the'

[10:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'last time you were on a discovery call or a sales call with a client. How much knowledge and information do you deliver just naturally asking answering people\'s questions? People are literally coming to you with their pain points starting with the pain and you\'re working with them to say how you can solve that for'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them. And that helps you build up that context. Again, find all those massive FAQs that people are asking, all those questions that they are asking in your business that are previously gone undocumented. You can start taking all'

[11:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that information. The other thing you can do is it can be the the start of automating whole processes. For me, like making uh proposals was always a pain because it\'s free unpaid work. Like I'

[11:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'couldn\'t really be bothered. There was paid work on the table and I\'m trying to win new business and it always got shunted down my list cuz I didn\'t know that a client might ghost me and it seemed like quite a big time sync for not guaranteed work. Now with a'

[11:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transcript, with the exact process of my business documented, with all my services and pricing and about my business documented, I can essentially take that transcript or get AI to go into my granola notetaking app, get that'

[11:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transcript. It knows how to write a proposal on my brand and with my services, and it can go and just create that all automatically. I actually went through that whole process in my previous YouTube videos step by step in'

[12:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'screen shares if you want to see like how would I pick a pain or a problem in my business and go and automate that step by step. I actually build you through that whole workflow. Now number four it\'s not really about using AI.'

[12:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s more about the mindset. And I want to tell you that if you\'re in a job where you\'ve sort of had a blanket no on AI or the company is slow to adopt it. This was something I had in my previous'

[12:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'role again that about 3 years ago the company just was not using AI. Um and they they sort of were poo pooing it. It just wasn\'t really done thing in the company. They had sort of had the line that that like AI it can\'t do what I do.'

[12:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It doesn\'t work. I want to tell you that the the number four big mistake is waiting for permission. Don\'t wait for other people\'s permission to start figuring this stuff out. Invest in yourself. This is going to pay off'

[12:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'massively over the next few months. We\'ve had students that have taken the the plunge to do this for themselves. They\'ve invested in the right tools and they\'ve invested the time and learning for themselves, invested in themselves.'

[13:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And they\'ve gone in a company from hey, no one uses AI here. which is not really the done thing to actually being given huge promotions, being able to go out on their own as consultants, start their own businesses cuz they are suddenly'

[13:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'light years ahead. They\'re in that 1%. So if your boss or your team or whatever aren\'t pro AI, don\'t say, "Oh, well, I can\'t use it." Take the time to learn this for yourself. Use it on your personal stuff. Use it on your own work.'

[13:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Use it on your side hustle or even use it on your own computer to execute work tasks and bring them back. Number one, that\'s going to give you huge freedom in your life at work. If you\'re taking hours off your plate of tasks that are to be done at your work, particularly if'

[13:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re in a remote role, you can claw hours of your life back. You can perform higher. You can get more done at work at a better level. Or you can spend time, you can have that more freedom of time to start your side hustle to come to the beach and go surfing in an afternoon'

[13:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because all the work\'s being done and no one\'s any the wiser. And what you\'ll find is within a few months, you\'ll be the person that\'s getting the promotion or you\'ll be the AI lead at that company because no one else has bothered to'

[14:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually take the plunge for themselves. They\'re all waiting for permission or their company to actually tell them how to do this fund this stuff. But if you want to get ahead, don\'t wait for other people to gatekeep this stuff for for you and let you do it. Take it on your'

[14:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'own shoulders to actually go and learn AI yourself. There is a cost associated. You might have to spend $100 extra a month on the AI tool. You might even have to invest in a course for yourself that you take in your free time in the evenings. But the payoff has never been'

[14:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so quick. You will find a payoff within a couple of weeks of doing this stuff for sure. There\'s no doubt about that. That\'s how quickly you can learn to actually use this stuff once you get some of these fundamentals that I\'m'

[14:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'talking about here down. Now, number five, it\'s actually bottom of my list here. It\'s number 13. It was the last thing I came up with here. Lucky number 13, which is huge. It is a level playing field for everyone. No one has 10 years'

[15:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of experience in AI. Chat GPT only came out in 2022. People have only really started using it in the last two years. So whether you\'re 20 years old and you\'re in college and you\'re you\'re trying to figure this stuff out, or'

[15:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'re semi-retired, you\'re 60 or 70 years old or anywhere in between, no one has 10 years head start on you. At best, they have two years of a head start on you. And a lot of those people, again, they\'re still sat in the 99%. They\'re'

[15:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just using AI to write emails quicker and and uh touch up their LinkedIn post comment on LinkedIn. So again, there\'s a level playing field here. No one no one has this skill that they\'ve developed'

[15:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'over 10 or 20 years. So just by going deeper and learning, spending time understanding how these tools work, how to build these systems underneath within, I\'d say less than a month, you can be one of these top AI users,'

[15:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'especially if you invest in the right plans. Most people are still running on the free plan. You need to invest also in the right plans. People are trying to automate their whole job and whole business and they\'re just using the free plan and saying, "Oh, this doesn\'t'

[16:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really work." It\'s like, yeah, you haven\'t really actually bought AI. You\'ve just used what\'s been given to you. And there\'s the old thing, you know, if you if the tool is free, then you are the product. You\'re just feeding AI data. Equally, if you\'re only using a $20 a month plan as well, I\'d say it'

[16:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will tap out pretty quickly and you\'ll get frustrated again and be like, "Oh, it doesn\'t work. I\'ve lost all my credits already. But think about it. If you\'re able to save yourself even two, three, four, five hours a week and you\'re paying for a $100 a month claude'

[16:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'max plan or a premium team seat, the ROI is incalculable because what happens, and this is another point on my list, is everything everything in your life becomes faster, everything works faster,'

[16:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and everything becomes easier because you\'re developing this new skill set. This is a whole new skill set that people have only had two years on. So far, it\'s a new skill set. It\'s a new mindset. And once you start viewing'

[16:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything you do through an AI lens, whether that\'s, I don\'t know, even planning a group holiday, instead of it being a slog and messages and back and forth and and keeping track of everything, you can cut through all of'

[17:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that with AI. That\'s a great use case that I found earlier this year was planning a group trip. Suddenly, I had full documentation. I had Claude able to answer questions in a in a WhatsApp group. I had itineraries planned out.'

[17:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything mapped out automatically. But equally, everything becomes easier at work and business. You\'re no longer spending 5 hours a day creating and managing spreadsheets and doing reporting. You turn up at your computer and the work is done. And every task you'

[17:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have, you suddenly log into this operating system that you\'ve built with the context underneath. Claude and Chat GPT are becoming operating systems. They browse the web for you. They execute tasks for you. They do everything for'

[17:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you. Um, and so having this new mindset of let me approach this with AI first will make everything in life much much easier, much much faster. If you\'re'

[17:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'trying to start your own side hustle, you\'re trying to start your own consultancy on the side, this is how it\'s going to work. This is how you\'re going to get ahead of everyone else. Rather than thinking, oh, I don\'t really have time because I\'ve got my my normal'

[18:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'job or I\'m trying to run my business or trying to do other things. Suddenly you\'ve got speed and ease on your side in everything you do. You\'ve got a co-pilot working on everything. You\'ve'

[18:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'essentially you can build a team of of AI employees, AI workers around you to start doing all of that work that you don\'t like doing. All of that work that you don\'t like doing. In my opinion, AI'

[18:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is here to empower us. It\'s here to amplify our best bits and cut through the drudgery. So all of that drudgery you do in your day-to-day work and life, you can get rid of that. You just got to'

[18:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'understand how to actually do that through the lens of AI with this new skill set. And that means you can spend more time doing what you love. For me, that means, hey, I don\'t have to spend at my previous job it was, hey, I don\'t have to spend 10, 20 hours a month on'

[18:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'SEO reporting because I could turn up at my computer and the reports were there ready to go for me to check through and send them off. I could spend more time talking to clients, selling new services to clients, connecting with them,'

[19:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'finding out their problems that I could solve for them, increasing the value to the people that I\'m working with. Or what I like to do, I used to always take half day Thursdays. I used to take Thursday afternoon off. I worked a remote job. I was getting all my'

[19:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'deliverables done, so I used to take off on a Thursday afternoon and and come and whatever, come to the beach, go play golf, surf, work on my side hustle, build my YouTube channel. can honestly say that I wouldn\'t be sat here talking to you. I wouldn\'t have built this'

[19:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'YouTube channel up without AI. I\'d tried in the past and it was such a slog. But now I have a system underneath that understands how to do all the boring stuff like write the the description like research competitors, do the data'

[19:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'analysis to bring everything to me on my plate so I can sit here and do what I love to do and actually teach. I can spend more time teaching which is one of my greatest strengths. I can sit here and create content. I don\'t get bogged down in spending hours, like 6 hours'

[20:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'editing. Used to take hours to edit videos. Now it takes half an hour because I\'ve got AI features that can autoedit videos for me. I\'m just putting the final touches and the tidying up the final clips for me at the end. And and I'

[20:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was even talking to uh a student the other day from from that 12 weeks and she\'d taken her YouTube sorry her podcast editing process which was a slog. It was a 6 hours, 5 6 hours'

[20:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'slog to do the main cut of her podcast, tidy it up, get that sort of 2-hour chat she might had down into 30 minutes. Now she\'s built a system which it takes her half 20 minutes, half an hour to not'

[20:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just edit and get it the right way. It actually analyzes the content, suggests ways that she can improve retention, improve her hooks, work with her back and forth, remember and analyze previous'

[20:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'episodes that had gone really well and the structure of those, and build frameworks and systems to make that repetitive. But it all started with what I talked about at the start of this video. Getting over the tool paralysis,'

[21:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'building systems and context and getting everything out of your brain, documenting every bit of a process so that you can attach it to AI. Working out what pain you had, which was I hate editing. It takes 6 hours. Why did it take 6 hours? Well, cuz I\'ve got to'

[21:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'upload. I\'ve got to scroll through. I\'ve got to read. I\'ve got to watch the whole video several times. Now, AI goes into the transcript. It works out the captions. It cuts out the right bits and all the retakes and all the stuff that'

[21:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just is waffle and not important and gives it back to her rearranged in the perfect order to help improve retention. So, she can now spend time recording more podcasts or coming up with ideas about how she can grow her business and'

